  message: "Categorizer v5 implemented and in flight (branch jgstern-agent/feat/wi-vupin-categorizer-v5). Rule count grew 8 → 46 categories with language-gated branching (Rust / Python / Java-Kotlin-Scala / Go / TS-JS). Offline re-aggregation of the 2026-04-11 per-repo JSONs (artifact: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/prospector_runs/run-2026-04-11-full/aggregate-v5.json) shows uncategorized rate 94.0% -> 43.4%, passing the <50% WI-tubot threshold. Iteration convergence observed at the 3pp bar (pass deltas: 27.2 / 11.1 / 6.2 / 3.7 / 2.3 pp) so further heuristic expansion halts here."  # 7ac1
- op: discuss  # 996a
  at: '2026-04-16T17:15:32Z'  # 996a
  by: agent  # 996a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 996a
  clock: 6  # 996a
  nonce: 996a  # 996a
  message: "REFLECTION on diminishing returns (per user request): why the 3pp threshold was crossed and what would be needed to categorize the residual 43.4%.\n\nWHY THE SLOWDOWN. The categorizer is a pure name/path regex — no access to the call graph, inheritance tree, framework registration points, or dynamic dispatch metadata. Each pass adds a fixed-size set of patterns against an ever-longer tail of unique class/method names. Pass 1 caught obvious structural categories (generated files, migrations, tests); pass 2 added framework lifecycle and JavaBean accessors; pass 3 refined Django ORM, Airflow framework, React lifecycle; pass 4 added narrow framework entry points (Airflow provider, OpenLineage, Apollo plugin); pass 5 added corpus-specific class-name rules (Cilium BPF, Kafka internals, wasmtime Inst descriptors). Each pass narrows faster because (a) the remaining tail spans thousands of distinct classes across diverse codebases, (b) names alone cannot distinguish 'missing linker edge' from 'genuine dead code' without the call graph, and (c) corpus-specific rules overfit — pass-5 Cilium BPF rules would not help on a non-Cilium cohort.\n\nALTERNATIVE STRATEGIES for the residual 43%:\n\n1. Consume hypergumbo analysis output, not strings. The dead-candidate record already has cross_language_hits and path_shape_boost. If hypergumbo emitted per-candidate unresolved-call edge targets (e.g., this symbol is referenced via an unresolved org.springframework.beans.factory.BeanFactory binding), the categorizer could partition by linker opportunity, not name shape. Requires schema addition to dead-code-maybe output.\n\n2. Class inheritance hierarchy. Analyzers already track 'implements'/'extends' — if inheritance reached the candidate record, residual methods like iterator.next, BaseOperator.execute, ServiceImpl.customize could be categorized by base type rather than by regex.\n\n3. Framework annotation provenance. Spring @Bean/@Autowired, Django @register, Airflow @dag/@task, Kubebuilder //+kubebuilder:*. Surface annotation metadata into candidates and ~10-20% of residual maps to 'registered via annotation — missing linker for registration machinery.'\n\n4. File-level co-occurrence propagation. Files with 5+ uncategorized candidates probably share a semantic (e.g., every method in BPFOps.go dispatches via BPF map ops). One-pass propagate-within-file after the per-candidate rules would catch methods whose names are too generic to pattern-match.\n\n5. LLM-assisted clustering. Sample residual, embed, cluster, propose categories. Higher-quality themes than heuristics but adds LLM dependency to bakeoff.\n\n6. Accept the structural limit. The point of the categorizer is to rank linker investment. At 56% labeled coverage, the top categories (cross_language_api, handler_or_dto, java_bean_accessor, python_airflow_framework) already dominate the pool and are steering the linker roadmap. Driving uncategorized toward 0% via narrow heuristics adds classification depth without adding decision value — the ranked output is already actionable. Strategies 1-3 would add real signal; strategies 4-5 need judgment calls on cost/benefit."  # 996a
